{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 22, :comments ["<b>עַשֵּׂר תְּעַשֵּׂר — you shall surely tithe.</b> This too is a commandment that he comes to explain, for it is said (Leviticus 27:30): \"And all the tithe of the land, of the seed of the land, of the fruit of the tree, is HaShem's; it is holy to HaShem,\" and it is said (ibid. v. 31): \"And if a man redeem any of his tithe, he shall add a fifth to it.\" Now this is not the tithe of the Levites, for concerning that it is said (Numbers 18:31): \"And you shall eat it in every place,\" and it is unconsecrated (חֻלִּין) — so what reason would there be for its redemption? But now he explains that He obligates him to tithe all the produce of your seed, which he himself and his sons shall eat before HaShem, in order that you may learn to fear HaShem — for the priests and the judges who stand there before HaShem, the teachers of the Torah, will teach him its fear and instruct him in the Torah and the commandments. And he explained the reason for the redemption that he mentioned there: because sometimes the tithe will be very great, for HaShem will bless you, and the journey will be too much for it, and it is better to redeem it for silver and bring the silver of the redemption up there. And he did not mention the fifth (חֹמֶשׁ), because it has already been mentioned.\n\nNow the meaning of \"the yield of the field\" — \"and all that emerges from the field\" — is as he said: \"of the seed of the land, of the fruit of the tree,\" that is, that which emerges from the field. And the intent of <b>כָּל תְּבוּאַת זַרְעֶךָ</b> (all the produce of your seed) is not that he must tithe all the produce of everything that he sows, nor all that emerges from the field year by year. Rather, the meaning of the verse is that regarding the species obligated in tithing — all his produce and all that emerges from them from the field — it admonishes him not to tithe from what comes into his hand of them partially and thereby exempt the remainder, but rather to tithe in good faith both what he measured out for sowing and what grew in excess of that. And afterward (Deuteronomy 14:23) he mentioned the species obligated to be tithed: \"the tithe of your grain (דָּגָן), your wine (תִּירֹשׁ), and your oil (יִצְהָר).\" And so he mentions in every place (Deuteronomy 12:17): \"You may not eat within your gates the tithe of your grain (דְּגָנְךָ), your wine (תִּירֹשְׁךָ), and your oil (וְיִצְהָרֶךָ),\" etc., and likewise he says in the passage of priestly gifts (Numbers 18:12): \"All the best of the oil (יִצְהָר), and all the best of the wine (תִּירוֹשׁ) and grain (דָּגָן), the first part of them which they give to HaShem\" — for these alone are obligated in תְּרוּמָה and tithes by Torah law. And likewise, what the verse says (Leviticus 27:30), \"And all the tithe of the land, of the seed of the land, of the fruit of the tree, is HaShem's\" — it does not command that he tithe every seed of the land and every fruit of the tree, but its meaning is: whatever you tithe of the seed of the land, which is דָּגָן, and of the fruit of the tree, which is תִּירוֹשׁ and יִצְהָר, shall be HaShem's. In this very language Rashi wrote there: \"of the seed of the land — דָּגָן; of the fruit of the tree — תִּירוֹשׁ and יִצְהָר.\" And the verse is abbreviated there, for it is not the place of the commandment to obligate tithing, but rather it is a commandment that the tithe be holy to HaShem until it is redeemed with the addition of a fifth. But the commandment regarding תְּרוּמוֹת and tithes everywhere applies to דָּגָן, תִּירוֹשׁ, and יִצְהָר.\n\nAnd the meaning of דָּגָן in the sacred tongue is the five known species of grain. And the meaning of תִּירוֹשׁ is the new wine that the vats yield. And יִצְהָר is the oil that comes from them. And no other species among all seeds and all fruits of the tree is obligated by Torah law at all — neither in תְּרוּמוֹת nor in tithes. And there are variant beraitot in Torat Kohanim (Bekhukotai, chapter 12:9) and in the Sifrei (Re'eh, 105) serving as supports for the matter, and they are mistaken. But the matter that is elucidated in the Gemara from the Babylonian Talmud (Berakhot 36a) and the Jerusalem Talmud (Challah 4:4) and the plain sense of the verse is thus: that even olives and grapes — their tithe is not by Torah law until they are made into תִּירוֹשׁ and יִצְהָר. And let this make you careful, for already the great codifiers have erred in it (see Rambam, Laws of Terumot 2:1).\n\nAnd the meaning of שָׁנָה שָׁנָה (year by year) is that he tithe it two years, one after the other — this is the tradition of our Sages (Rosh HaShanah 12b). And afterward (Deuteronomy 14:28) he explained: \"At the end of three years you shall bring forth the tithe of your produce\" of that year and give it to the poor — to say that the third year is the year of the poor man's tithe. And he said: \"you shall bring forth… and lay it up within your gates,\" to teach the law of removal (בִּעוּר), and he will explain it again in the passage \"When you have finished tithing\" (Deuteronomy 26:12).\n\nAnd the midrash of the Sages (Tanhuma, Re'eh 18) expounds the expression עַשֵּׂר תְּעַשֵּׂר as: tithe so that you may become rich (תִּתְעַשֵּׁר); tithe so that you shall not lack — a hint for those of the distant isles, to remove one of ten for the toilers in Torah."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 14, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:14:49.271060206Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}